  "output": {
    "stories": [
      {
        "editor_spans": [
          {
            "body": "The full speech changes what I wrote last week, and I should say so. I said Burnham's offer looked like a way round Edinburgh: Westminster building direct links with Scottish regions and treating Holyrood as one more centre to bypass. The concrete offer points the other way. Employment support run by the DWP is reserved to Westminster, and he is offering a Scottish version of it 'shaped by Holyrood'. That is real power going to the Scottish Government first. The criticism of Holyrood's centralism is still there, but the actual deal goes through Edinburgh, not around it.",
            "cites": [
              355,
              305,
              306,
              307
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "His best example also cuts against his own argument. He calls Scotland one of the most centralised nations in Europe, yet the idea he admires most, the 'Easterhouse principle' of names not numbers, came out of Scotland. He reached FARE-Scotland through the Scottish Violence Reduction Unit, a national body. In other words, the centralised Scottish system he criticises produced the bottom-up work he wants to copy. What matters is whether a central body gives local groups room and steady funding. A new tier of government doesn't guarantee that, and lacking one doesn't rule it out.",
            "cites": [
              36,
              153,
              242,
              310,
              315
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The bigger problem is his account of deindustrialisation. He blames Ravenscraig and Leigh on decisions 'taken hundreds of miles away' by people who would never live with the consequences. Then the answer he offers is more decision-making for local areas. But local control over skills, transport and innovation funds could not have kept a steelworks or a coalfield open. Those closures came from national choices about ownership, energy and industrial strategy. Moving decisions down to regions won't stop the next closure. Making different decisions at the centre might.",
            "cites": [
              82,
              84,
              85,
              90,
              91,
              92,
              98,
              317
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "There is a live test of this in the speech itself. Aberdeen gets one sentence: people there are 'living through that now', and he will 'have more to say about that in due course'. The decline of the North Sea oil and gas industry is a closure on the scale of Ravenscraig, and the big levers on it — licensing, tax and the pace of the energy transition — are in Westminster's hands. If he means his line that no one in Whitehall planned a transition for Leigh, Aberdeen is where to prove it. Pushing power to the regions doesn't help with that. It needs the centre to act. Against it, his concrete money is a £10 million share of two defence colleges and support for Grangemouth whose details are still to come.",
            "cites": [
              108,
              92,
              175,
              183,
              185
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
